1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:08,865
EVERY object in the next day’s journey was new and interesting to Elizabeth; and her spirits

2
00:00:08,865 --> 00:00:13,066
were in a state of enjoyment; for she had seen her sister looking so well as to banish

3
00:00:13,066 --> 00:00:18,000
all fear for her health, and the prospect of her northern tour was a constant source of delight.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,600
When they left the high road for the lane to Hunsford, every eye was in search of

5
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:27,532
the Parsonage, and every turning expected to bring it in view. The paling of Rosings

6
00:00:27,532 --> 00:00:31,500
park was their boundary on one side. Elizabeth smiled at the recollection

7
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:36,966
of all that she had heard of its inhabitants. At length the Parsonage was discernible. The

8
00:00:36,966 --> 00:00:41,799
garden sloping to the road, the house standing in it, the green pales and the laurel hedge,

9
00:00:41,799 --> 00:00:46,665
everything declared they were arriving. Mr. Collins and Charlotte appeared at the door,

10
00:00:46,665 --> 00:00:50,933
and the carriage stopped at the small gate, which led by a short gravel walk to the house,

11
00:00:50,933 --> 00:00:56,333
amidst the nods and smiles of the whole party. In a moment they were all out of the chaise,

12
00:00:56,333 --> 00:01:01,265
rejoicing at the sight of each other. Mrs. Collins welcomed her friend with the liveliest pleasure,

13
00:01:01,265 --> 00:01:05,632
and Elizabeth was more and more satisfied with coming, when she found herself so affectionately

14
00:01:05,632 --> 00:01:10,899
received. She saw instantly that her cousin’s manners were not altered by his marriage: his

15
00:01:10,899 --> 00:01:16,000
formal civility was just what it had been; and he detained her some minutes at the gate to hear and

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,233
satisfy his inquiries after all her family. They were then, with no other delay than his pointing

17
00:01:21,233 --> 00:01:25,566
out the neatness of the entrance, taken into the house; and as soon as they were in the parlour,

18
00:01:25,566 --> 00:01:31,299
he welcomed them a second time, with ostentatious formality, to his humble abode, and punctually

19
00:01:31,299 --> 00:01:36,700
repeated all his wife’s offers of refreshment. Elizabeth was prepared to see him in his glory;

20
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:40,265
and she could not help fancying that in displaying the good proportion of the room,

21
00:01:40,265 --> 00:01:45,966
its aspect, and its furniture, he addressed himself particularly to her, as if wishing to

22
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:51,100
make her feel what she had lost in refusing him. But though everything seemed neat and comfortable,

23
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:56,333
she was not able to gratify him by any sigh of repentance; and rather looked with wonder at

24
00:01:56,333 --> 00:02:01,132
her friend, that she could have so cheerful an air with such a companion. When Mr. Collins said

25
00:02:01,132 --> 00:02:05,566
anything of which his wife might reasonably be ashamed, which certainly was not seldom,

26
00:02:05,566 --> 00:02:10,600
she involuntarily turned her eye on Charlotte. Once or twice she could discern a faint blush;

27
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:16,500
but in general Charlotte wisely did not hear. After sitting long enough to admire every article

28
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:20,700
of furniture in the room, from the sideboard to the fender, to give an account of their journey,

29
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:24,899
and of all that had happened in London, Mr. Collins invited them to take a stroll in the

30
00:02:24,899 --> 00:02:29,799
garden, which was large and well laid out, and to the cultivation of which he attended himself.

31
00:02:29,799 --> 00:02:34,799
To work in his garden was one of his most respectable pleasures; and Elizabeth admired

32
00:02:34,799 --> 00:02:39,399
the command of countenance with which Charlotte talked of the healthfulness of the exercise, and

33
00:02:39,399 --> 00:02:45,100
owned she encouraged it as much as possible. Here, leading the way through every walk and cross walk,

34
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:50,100
and scarcely allowing them an interval to utter the praises he asked for, every view was pointed

35
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:54,899
out with a minuteness which left beauty entirely behind. He could number the fields in every

36
00:02:54,899 --> 00:03:00,066
direction, and could tell how many trees there were in the most distant clump. But of all the

37
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:04,433
views which his garden, or which the country or the kingdom could boast, none were to be compared

38
00:03:04,433 --> 00:03:09,665
with the prospect of Rosings, afforded by an opening in the trees that bordered the park nearly

39
00:03:09,665 --> 00:03:15,299
opposite the front of his house. It was a handsome modern building, well situated on rising ground.

40
00:03:16,265 --> 00:03:20,765
From his garden, Mr. Collins would have led them round his two meadows; but the ladies,

41
00:03:20,765 --> 00:03:25,665
not having shoes to encounter the remains of a white frost, turned back; and while Sir William

42
00:03:25,665 --> 00:03:30,665
accompanied him, Charlotte took her sister and friend over the house, extremely well pleased,

43
00:03:30,665 --> 00:03:35,399
probably, to have the opportunity of showing it without her husband’s help. It was rather small,

44
00:03:35,399 --> 00:03:40,000
but well built and convenient; and everything was fitted up and arranged with a neatness

45
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,333
and consistency, of which Elizabeth gave Charlotte all the credit. When

46
00:03:44,333 --> 00:03:48,365
Mr. Collins could be forgotten, there was really a great air of comfort throughout,

47
00:03:48,365 --> 00:03:53,700
and by Charlotte’s evident enjoyment of it, Elizabeth supposed he must be often forgotten.

48
00:03:54,399 --> 00:03:58,433
She had already learnt that Lady Catherine was still in the country. It was spoken of

49
00:03:58,433 --> 00:04:02,032
again while they were at dinner, when Mr. Collins joining in, observed,—

50
00:04:02,032 --> 00:04:06,833
“Yes, Miss Elizabeth, you will have the honour of seeing Lady Catherine de Bourgh

51
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:13,200
on the ensuing Sunday at church, and I need not say you will be delighted with her. She is all

52
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:18,100
affability and condescension, and I doubt not but you will be honoured with some portion of

53
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:23,333
her notice when service is over. I have scarcely any hesitation in saying that she will include

54
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:29,200
you and my sister Maria in every invitation with which she honours us during your stay here. Her

55
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:35,632
behaviour to my dear Charlotte is charming. We dine at Rosings twice every week, and are never

56
00:04:35,632 --> 00:04:43,000
allowed to walk home. Her Ladyship’s carriage is regularly ordered for us. I should say, one of her

57
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,399
Ladyship’s carriages, for she has several.” “Lady Catherine is a very respectable,

58
00:04:48,399 --> 00:04:52,433
sensible woman, indeed,” added Charlotte, “and a most attentive neighbour.”

59
00:04:53,032 --> 00:04:59,265
“Very true, my dear, that is exactly what I say. She is the sort of woman whom one

60
00:04:59,265 --> 00:05:04,966
cannot regard with too much deference.” The evening was spent chiefly in talking

61
00:05:04,966 --> 00:05:09,899
over Hertfordshire news, and telling again what had been already written; and when it closed,

62
00:05:09,899 --> 00:05:14,933
Elizabeth, in the solitude of her chamber, had to meditate upon Charlotte’s degree of contentment,

63
00:05:14,933 --> 00:05:19,865
to understand her address in guiding, and composure in bearing with, her husband, and

64
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:25,299
to acknowledge that it was all done very well. She had also to anticipate how her visit would pass,

65
00:05:25,299 --> 00:05:30,365
the quiet tenour of their usual employments, the vexatious interruptions of Mr. Collins,

66
00:05:30,365 --> 00:05:36,466
and the gaieties of their intercourse with Rosings. A lively imagination soon settled it all.

67
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:39,899
About the middle of the next day, as she was in her room getting ready for a walk,

68
00:05:39,899 --> 00:05:45,000
a sudden noise below seemed to speak the whole house in confusion; and, after listening a moment,

69
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,165
she heard somebody running upstairs in a violent hurry, and calling loudly after her. She opened

70
00:05:50,165 --> 00:05:55,466
the door, and met Maria in the landing-place, who, breathless with agitation, cried out,—

71
00:05:55,532 --> 00:05:58,365
“Oh, my dear Eliza! pray make haste

72
00:05:58,365 --> 00:06:02,333
and come into the dining-room, for there is such a sight to be seen! I will not tell you what it is.

73
00:06:02,333 --> 00:06:06,833
Make haste, and come down this moment.” Elizabeth asked questions in vain;

74
00:06:06,833 --> 00:06:11,333
Maria would tell her nothing more; and down they ran into the dining-room which fronted the lane,

75
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:16,000
in quest of this wonder; it was two ladies, stopping in a low phaeton at the garden gate.

76
00:06:16,566 --> 00:06:21,833
“And is this all?” cried Elizabeth. “I expected at least that the pigs were got into the garden,

77
00:06:21,833 --> 00:06:24,299
and here is nothing but Lady Catherine and her daughter!”

78
00:06:25,365 --> 00:06:30,100
“La! my dear,” said Maria, quite shocked at the mistake, “it is not Lady Catherine.

79
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:35,333
The old lady is Mrs. Jenkinson, who lives with them. The other is Miss De Bourgh. Only look at

80
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:39,899
her. She is quite a little creature. Who would have thought she could be so thin and small!”

81
00:06:40,566 --> 00:06:45,466
“She is abominably rude to keep Charlotte out of doors in all this wind. Why does she not come in?”

82
00:06:46,066 --> 00:06:47,865
“Oh, Charlotte says she hardly ever does.

83
00:06:47,865 --> 00:06:50,933
It is the greatest of favours when Miss De Bourgh comes in.”

84
00:06:51,466 --> 00:06:56,765
“I like her appearance,” said Elizabeth, struck with other ideas. “She looks sickly

85
00:06:56,765 --> 00:07:02,500
and cross. Yes, she will do for him very well. She will make him a very proper wife.”

86
00:07:03,532 --> 00:07:08,265
Mr. Collins and Charlotte were both standing at the gate in conversation with the ladies; and

87
00:07:08,265 --> 00:07:13,599
Sir William, to Elizabeth’s high diversion, was stationed in the doorway, in earnest contemplation

88
00:07:13,599 --> 00:07:18,065
of the greatness before him, and constantly bowing whenever Miss De Bourgh looked that way.

89
00:07:18,966 --> 00:07:23,099
At length there was nothing more to be said; the ladies drove on, and the others returned

90
00:07:23,099 --> 00:07:28,265
into the house. Mr. Collins no sooner saw the two girls than he began to congratulate them on their

91
00:07:28,265 --> 00:07:33,000
good fortune, which Charlotte explained by letting them know that the whole party was asked to dine

92
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,099
at Rosings the next day.

93
00:07:35,099 --> 00:07:37,065
“Lady Catherine, said she, you have given me a treasure.”

94
00:07:37,065 --> 00:07:38,200
CHAPTER XXIX.

95
00:07:44,799 --> 00:07:50,700
MR. COLLINS’S triumph, in consequence of this invitation, was complete. The power of

96
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:54,900
displaying the grandeur of his patroness to his wondering visitors, and of letting them

97
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:59,466
see her civility towards himself and his wife, was exactly what he had wished for;

98
00:07:59,466 --> 00:08:04,599
and that an opportunity of doing it should be given so soon was such an instance of

99
00:08:04,599 --> 00:08:08,332
Lady Catherine’s condescension as he knew not how to admire enough.

100
00:08:09,532 --> 00:08:16,599
“I confess,” said he, “that I should not have been at all surprised by her Ladyship’s asking

101
00:08:16,599 --> 00:08:22,065
us on Sunday to drink tea and spend the evening at Rosings. I rather expected,

102
00:08:22,065 --> 00:08:28,565
from my knowledge of her affability, that it would happen. But who could have foreseen such

103
00:08:28,565 --> 00:08:33,532
an attention as this? Who could have imagined that we should receive an invitation to dine

104
00:08:33,532 --> 00:08:39,765
there (an invitation, moreover, including the whole party) so immediately after your arrival?”

105
00:08:40,733 --> 00:08:44,265
“I am the less surprised at what has happened,” replied Sir William,

106
00:08:44,265 --> 00:08:48,832
“from that knowledge of what the manners of the great really are, which my situation in life

107
00:08:48,832 --> 00:08:54,400
has allowed me to acquire. About the court, such instances of elegant breeding are not uncommon.”

108
00:08:55,366 --> 00:09:00,532
Scarcely anything was talked of the whole day or next morning but their visit to Rosings.

109
00:09:00,532 --> 00:09:06,400
Mr. Collins was carefully instructing them in what they were to expect, that the sight of such rooms,

110
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:10,900
so many servants, and so splendid a dinner, might not wholly overpower them.

111
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:14,765
When the ladies were separating for the toilette, he said to Elizabeth,—

112
00:09:14,765 --> 00:09:21,900
“Do not make yourself uneasy, my dear cousin, about your apparel. Lady Catherine is far from

113
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:27,765
requiring that elegance of dress in us which becomes herself and daughter. I would advise

114
00:09:27,765 --> 00:09:31,866
you merely to put on whatever of your clothes is superior to the rest—there

115
00:09:31,866 --> 00:09:36,599
is no occasion for anything more. Lady Catherine will not think the worse of

116
00:09:36,599 --> 00:09:42,065
you for being simply dressed. She likes to have the distinction of rank preserved.”

117
00:09:42,966 --> 00:09:47,765
While they were dressing, he came two or three times to their different doors, to recommend

118
00:09:47,765 --> 00:09:52,299
their being quick, as Lady Catherine very much objected to be kept waiting for her dinner.

119
00:09:52,866 --> 00:09:58,265
Such formidable accounts of her Ladyship, and her manner of living, quite frightened Maria Lucas,

120
00:09:58,265 --> 00:10:02,332
who had been little used to company; and she looked forward to her introduction at Rosings

121
00:10:02,332 --> 00:10:06,966
with as much apprehension as her father had done to his presentation at St. James’s.

122
00:10:07,565 --> 00:10:11,933
As the weather was fine, they had a pleasant walk of about half a mile across the park.

123
00:10:11,933 --> 00:10:17,500
Every park has its beauty and its prospects; and Elizabeth saw much to be pleased with,

124
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:22,133
though she could not be in such raptures as Mr. Collins expected the scene to inspire,

125
00:10:22,133 --> 00:10:27,466
and was but slightly affected by his enumeration of the windows in front of the house, and his

126
00:10:27,466 --> 00:10:31,433
relation of what the glazing altogether had originally cost Sir Lewis de Bourgh.

127
00:10:31,966 --> 00:10:37,733
When they ascended the steps to the hall, Maria’s alarm was every moment increasing, and even Sir

128
00:10:37,733 --> 00:10:43,599
William did not look perfectly calm. Elizabeth’s courage did not fail her. She had heard nothing of

129
00:10:43,599 --> 00:10:47,933
Lady Catherine that spoke her awful from any extraordinary talents or miraculous virtue,

130
00:10:47,933 --> 00:10:52,599
and the mere stateliness of money and rank she thought she could witness without trepidation.

131
00:10:53,565 --> 00:10:57,700
From the entrance hall, of which Mr. Collins pointed out, with a rapturous air,

132
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:02,500
the fine proportion and finished ornaments, they followed the servants through an antechamber to

133
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:07,200
the room where Lady Catherine, her daughter, and Mrs. Jenkinson were sitting. Her Ladyship,

134
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:11,933
with great condescension, arose to receive them; and as Mrs. Collins had settled it with her

135
00:11:11,933 --> 00:11:16,433
husband that the office of introduction should be hers, it was performed in a proper manner,

136
00:11:16,433 --> 00:11:20,166
without any of those apologies and thanks which he would have thought necessary.

137
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:25,133
In spite of having been at St. James’s, Sir William was so completely awed by the

138
00:11:25,133 --> 00:11:29,166
grandeur surrounding him, that he had but just courage enough to make a very low bow,

139
00:11:29,166 --> 00:11:34,500
and take his seat without saying a word; and his daughter, frightened almost out of her senses,

140
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:40,265
sat on the edge of her chair, not knowing which way to look. Elizabeth found herself quite equal

141
00:11:40,265 --> 00:11:46,000
to the scene, and could observe the three ladies before her composedly. Lady Catherine was a tall,

142
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:51,599
large woman, with strongly-marked features, which might once have been handsome. Her air

143
00:11:51,599 --> 00:11:56,032
was not conciliating, nor was her manner of receiving them such as to make her visitors

144
00:11:56,032 --> 00:12:01,133
forget their inferior rank. She was not rendered formidable by silence: but whatever

145
00:12:01,133 --> 00:12:06,400
she said was spoken in so authoritative a tone as marked her self-importance, and brought Mr.

146
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:10,799
Wickham immediately to Elizabeth’s mind; and, from the observation of the day altogether,

147
00:12:10,799 --> 00:12:14,466
she believed Lady Catherine to be exactly what he had represented.

148
00:12:15,099 --> 00:12:20,332
When, after examining the mother, in whose countenance and deportment she soon found

149
00:12:20,332 --> 00:12:24,832
some resemblance of Mr. Darcy, she turned her eyes on the daughter, she could almost have

150
00:12:24,832 --> 00:12:29,633
joined in Maria’s astonishment at her being so thin and so small. There was neither in figure

151
00:12:29,633 --> 00:12:35,565
nor face any likeness between the ladies. Miss de Bourgh was pale and sickly: her features,

152
00:12:35,565 --> 00:12:40,733
though not plain, were insignificant; and she spoke very little, except in a low voice,

153
00:12:40,733 --> 00:12:45,166
to Mrs. Jenkinson, in whose appearance there was nothing remarkable, and who was entirely

154
00:12:45,166 --> 00:12:50,032
engaged in listening to what she said, and placing a screen in the proper direction before her eyes.

155
00:12:50,633 --> 00:12:54,832
After sitting a few minutes, they were all sent to one of the windows to admire the view,

156
00:12:54,832 --> 00:12:57,900
Mr. Collins attending them to point out its beauties,

157
00:12:57,900 --> 00:13:02,633
and Lady Catherine kindly informing them that it was much better worth looking at in the summer.

158
00:13:03,666 --> 00:13:06,832
The dinner was exceedingly handsome, and there were all the servants,

159
00:13:06,832 --> 00:13:11,466
and all the articles of plate which Mr. Collins had promised; and, as he had likewise foretold,

160
00:13:11,466 --> 00:13:16,265
he took his seat at the bottom of the table, by her Ladyship’s desire, and looked as if he felt

161
00:13:16,265 --> 00:13:21,832
that life could furnish nothing greater. He carved and ate and praised with delighted alacrity;

162
00:13:21,832 --> 00:13:27,466
and every dish was commended first by him, and then by Sir William, who was now enough

163
00:13:27,466 --> 00:13:31,366
recovered to echo whatever his son-in-law said, in a manner which Elizabeth wondered

164
00:13:31,366 --> 00:13:36,299
Lady Catherine could bear. But Lady Catherine seemed gratified by their excessive admiration,

165
00:13:36,299 --> 00:13:42,599
and gave most gracious smiles, especially when any dish on the table proved a novelty to them.

166
00:13:42,599 --> 00:13:47,466
The party did not supply much conversation. Elizabeth was ready to speak whenever there was

167
00:13:47,466 --> 00:13:52,366
an opening, but she was seated between Charlotte and Miss de Bourgh—the former of whom was engaged

168
00:13:52,366 --> 00:13:56,500
in listening to Lady Catherine, and the latter said not a word to her all the dinnertime.

169
00:13:57,099 --> 00:14:02,166
Mrs. Jenkinson was chiefly employed in watching how little Miss de Bourgh ate, pressing her to try

170
00:14:02,166 --> 00:14:07,133
some other dish and fearing she was indisposed. Maria thought speaking out of the question,

171
00:14:07,133 --> 00:14:12,966
and the gentlemen did nothing but eat and admire. When the ladies returned to the drawing-room,

172
00:14:12,966 --> 00:14:17,400
there was little to be done but to hear Lady Catherine talk, which she did without any

173
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:22,200
intermission till coffee came in, delivering her opinion on every subject in so decisive a

174
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:26,933
manner as proved that she was not used to have her judgment controverted. She inquired into

175
00:14:26,933 --> 00:14:31,265
Charlotte’s domestic concerns familiarly and minutely, and gave her a great deal of advice

176
00:14:31,265 --> 00:14:35,166
as to the management of them all; told her how everything ought to be regulated in so

177
00:14:35,166 --> 00:14:39,366
small a family as hers, and instructed her as to the care of her cows and her poultry.

178
00:14:39,366 --> 00:14:43,866
Elizabeth found that nothing was beneath this great lady’s attention which could furnish her

179
00:14:43,866 --> 00:14:48,166
with an occasion for dictating to others. In the intervals of her discourse with Mrs. Collins,

180
00:14:48,166 --> 00:14:52,900
she addressed a variety of questions to Maria and Elizabeth, but especially to the latter,

181
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:57,900
of whose connections she knew the least, and who, she observed to Mrs. Collins, was a very genteel,

182
00:14:57,900 --> 00:15:02,633
pretty kind of girl. She asked her at different times how many sisters she had, whether they were

183
00:15:02,633 --> 00:15:06,666
older or younger than herself, whether any of them were likely to be married, whether

184
00:15:06,666 --> 00:15:11,032
they were handsome, where they had been educated, what carriage her father kept, and what had been

185
00:15:11,032 --> 00:15:16,599
her mother’s maiden name? Elizabeth felt all the impertinence of her questions, but answered them

186
00:15:16,599 --> 00:15:23,466
very composedly. Lady Catherine then observed,— “Your father’s estate is entailed on Mr. Collins,

187
00:15:23,466 --> 00:15:31,265
I think? For your sake,” turning to Charlotte, “I am glad of it; but otherwise I see no occasion for

188
00:15:31,265 --> 00:15:37,299
entailing estates from the female line. It was not thought necessary in Sir Lewis de Bourgh’s family.

189
00:15:38,032 --> 00:15:41,799
Do you play and sing, Miss Bennet?” “A little.”

190
00:15:42,366 --> 00:15:48,966
“Oh then—some time or other we shall be happy to hear you. Our instrument is a capital one,

191
00:15:49,599 --> 00:15:56,400
probably superior to —— you shall try it some day. Do your sisters play and sing?”

192
00:15:57,166 --> 00:16:02,332
“One of them does.” “Why did not you all learn? You ought all to have

193
00:16:02,332 --> 00:16:09,765
learned. The Miss Webbs all play, and their father has not so good an income as yours. Do you draw?”

194
00:16:10,500 --> 00:16:14,466
“No, not at all.” “What, none of you?”

195
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:21,065
“Not one.” “That is very strange. But I suppose you had

196
00:16:21,065 --> 00:16:27,366
no opportunity. Your mother should have taken you to town every spring for the benefit of masters.”

197
00:16:28,265 --> 00:16:32,099
“My mother would have no objection, but my father hates London.”

198
00:16:32,933 --> 00:16:37,065
“Has your governess left you?” “We never had any governess.”

199
00:16:37,966 --> 00:16:44,765
“No governess! How was that possible? Five daughters brought up at home without a governess!

200
00:16:44,765 --> 00:16:50,466
I never heard of such a thing. Your mother must have been quite a slave to your education.”

201
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:55,599
Elizabeth could hardly help smiling, as she assured her that had not been the case.

202
00:16:56,400 --> 00:17:02,466
“Then who taught you? who attended to you? Without a governess, you must have been neglected.”

203
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:08,400
“Compared with some families, I believe we were; but such of us as wished to learn never wanted

204
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:13,299
the means. We were always encouraged to read, and had all the masters that were necessary.

205
00:17:13,299 --> 00:17:19,966
Those who chose to be idle certainly might.” “Ay, no doubt: but that is what a governess

206
00:17:19,966 --> 00:17:25,900
will prevent; and if I had known your mother, I should have advised her most strenuously to engage

207
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:31,599
one. I always say that nothing is to be done in education without steady and regular instruction,

208
00:17:31,599 --> 00:17:38,099
and nobody but a governess can give it. It is wonderful how many families I have been

209
00:17:38,099 --> 00:17:45,366
the means of supplying in that way. I am always glad to get a young person well placed out. Four

210
00:17:45,366 --> 00:17:51,000
nieces of Mrs. Jenkinson are most delightfully situated through my means; and it was but the

211
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,966
other day that I recommended another young person, who was merely accidentally mentioned to me,

212
00:17:55,966 --> 00:18:02,565
and the family are quite delighted with her. Mrs. Collins, did I tell you of Lady Metcalfe’s

213
00:18:02,565 --> 00:18:09,532
calling yesterday to thank me? She finds Miss Pope a treasure. ‘Lady Catherine,’ said she,

214
00:18:09,532 --> 00:18:16,065
‘you have given me a treasure.’ Are any of your younger sisters out, Miss Bennet?”

215
00:18:16,733 --> 00:18:18,166
“Yes, ma’am, all.” “All!

216
00:18:20,500 --> 00:18:29,400
What, all five out at once? Very odd! And you only the second. The younger ones out

217
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:34,565
before the elder are married! Your younger sisters must be very young?”

218
00:18:35,466 --> 00:18:42,233
“Yes, my youngest is not sixteen. Perhaps she is full young to be much in company. But really,

219
00:18:42,233 --> 00:18:46,265
ma’am, I think it would be very hard upon younger sisters that they should not have their share of

220
00:18:46,265 --> 00:18:51,466
society and amusement, because the elder may not have the means or inclination to marry early. The

221
00:18:51,466 --> 00:18:56,466
last born has as good a right to the pleasures of youth as the first. And to be kept back on such

222
00:18:56,466 --> 00:19:01,366
a motive! I think it would not be very likely to promote sisterly affection or delicacy of mind.”

223
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:10,265
“Upon my word,” said her Ladyship, “you give your opinion very decidedly for so young a person.

224
00:19:10,966 --> 00:19:15,099
Pray, what is your age?” “With three younger sisters

225
00:19:15,099 --> 00:19:20,332
grown up,” replied Elizabeth, smiling, “your Ladyship can hardly expect me to own it.”

226
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,666
Lady Catherine seemed quite astonished at not receiving a direct answer;

227
00:19:24,666 --> 00:19:28,900
and Elizabeth suspected herself to be the first creature who had ever dared to trifle

228
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:34,500
with so much dignified impertinence. “You cannot be more than twenty, I am

229
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:39,900
sure,—therefore you need not conceal your age.” “I am not one-and-twenty.”

230
00:19:40,666 --> 00:19:45,966
When the gentlemen had joined them, and tea was over, the card tables were placed. Lady Catherine,

231
00:19:45,966 --> 00:19:50,933
Sir William, and Mr. and Mrs. Collins sat down to quadrille; and as Miss De Bourgh chose to play at

232
00:19:50,933 --> 00:19:56,332
cassino, the two girls had the honour of assisting Mrs. Jenkinson to make up her party. Their table

233
00:19:56,332 --> 00:20:01,966
was superlatively stupid. Scarcely a syllable was uttered that did not relate to the game, except

234
00:20:01,966 --> 00:20:07,200
when Mrs. Jenkinson expressed her fears of Miss De Bourgh’s being too hot or too cold, or having too

235
00:20:07,200 --> 00:20:13,133
much or too little light. A great deal more passed at the other table. Lady Catherine was generally

236
00:20:13,133 --> 00:20:18,966
speaking—stating the mistakes of the three others, or relating some anecdote of herself. Mr.

237
00:20:18,966 --> 00:20:24,000
Collins was employed in agreeing to everything her Ladyship said, thanking her for every fish he won,

238
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:29,799
and apologizing if he thought he won too many. Sir William did not say much. He was storing

239
00:20:29,799 --> 00:20:35,400
his memory with anecdotes and noble names. When Lady Catherine and her daughter had played

240
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:39,700
as long as they chose, the tables were broken up, the carriage was offered to Mrs. Collins,

241
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:45,065
gratefully accepted, and immediately ordered. The party then gathered round the fire to hear Lady

242
00:20:45,065 --> 00:20:49,299
Catherine determine what weather they were to have on the morrow. From these instructions they were

243
00:20:49,299 --> 00:20:53,166
summoned by the arrival of the coach; and with many speeches of thankfulness on Mr. Collins’s

244
00:20:53,166 --> 00:20:58,200
side, and as many bows on Sir William’s, they departed. As soon as they had driven from the

245
00:20:58,200 --> 00:21:02,332
door, Elizabeth was called on by her cousin to give her opinion of all that she had seen at

246
00:21:02,332 --> 00:21:06,599
Rosings, which, for Charlotte’s sake, she made more favourable than it really was.

247
00:21:06,599 --> 00:21:11,933
But her commendation, though costing her some trouble, could by no means satisfy Mr. Collins,

248
00:21:11,933 --> 00:21:16,799
and he was very soon obliged to take her Ladyship’s praise into his own hands.

249
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:20,032
CHAPTER XXX.

250
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:31,599
SIR WILLIAM stayed only a week at Hunsford; but his visit was long enough to convince him of his

251
00:21:31,599 --> 00:21:36,065
daughter’s being most comfortably settled, and of her possessing such a husband and such a neighbour

252
00:21:36,065 --> 00:21:41,332
as were not often met with. While Sir William was with them, Mr. Collins devoted his mornings

253
00:21:41,332 --> 00:21:46,299
to driving him out in his gig, and showing him the country: but when he went away, the whole family

254
00:21:46,299 --> 00:21:50,933
returned to their usual employments, and Elizabeth was thankful to find that they did not see more of

255
00:21:50,933 --> 00:21:55,565
her cousin by the alteration; for the chief of the time between breakfast and dinner was now passed

256
00:21:55,565 --> 00:22:00,700
by him either at work in the garden, or in reading and writing, and looking out of window in his own

257
00:22:00,700 --> 00:22:06,599
book room, which fronted the road. The room in which the ladies sat was backwards. Elizabeth at

258
00:22:06,599 --> 00:22:10,000
first had rather wondered that Charlotte should not prefer the dining parlour for common use;

259
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:16,065
it was a better sized room, and had a pleasanter aspect: but she soon saw that her friend had an

260
00:22:16,065 --> 00:22:20,933
excellent reason for what she did, for Mr. Collins would undoubtedly have been much less in his own

261
00:22:20,933 --> 00:22:26,332
apartment had they sat in one equally lively; and she gave Charlotte credit for the arrangement.

262
00:22:26,933 --> 00:22:30,966
From the drawing-room they could distinguish nothing in the lane, and were indebted to Mr.

263
00:22:30,966 --> 00:22:35,099
Collins for the knowledge of what carriages went along, and how often especially Miss

264
00:22:35,099 --> 00:22:38,765
De Bourgh drove by in her phaeton, which he never failed coming to inform them of,

265
00:22:38,765 --> 00:22:43,366
though it happened almost every day. She not unfrequently stopped at the Parsonage, and

266
00:22:43,366 --> 00:22:47,633
had a few minutes’ conversation with Charlotte, but was scarcely ever prevailed on to get out.

267
00:22:47,633 --> 00:22:53,799
Very few days passed in which Mr. Collins did not walk to Rosings, and not many in which his wife

268
00:22:53,799 --> 00:22:58,433
did not think it necessary to go likewise; and till Elizabeth recollected that there might be

269
00:22:58,433 --> 00:23:04,065
other family livings to be disposed of, she could not understand the sacrifice of so many hours. Now

270
00:23:04,065 --> 00:23:08,332
and then they were honoured with a call from her Ladyship, and nothing escaped her observation that

271
00:23:08,332 --> 00:23:12,765
was passing in the room during these visits. She examined into their employments, looked at

272
00:23:12,765 --> 00:23:17,265
their work, and advised them to do it differently; found fault with the arrangement of the furniture,

273
00:23:17,265 --> 00:23:22,799
or detected the housemaid in negligence; and if she accepted any refreshment, seemed to do it only

274
00:23:22,799 --> 00:23:26,866
for the sake of finding out that Mrs. Collins’s joints of meat were too large for her family.

275
00:23:26,866 --> 00:23:32,466
Elizabeth soon perceived, that though this great lady was not in the commission of the peace for

276
00:23:32,466 --> 00:23:37,500
the county, she was a most active magistrate in her own parish, the minutest concerns of

277
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:41,565
which were carried to her by Mr. Collins; and whenever any of the cottagers were disposed to

278
00:23:41,565 --> 00:23:46,565
be quarrelsome, discontented, or too poor, she sallied forth into the village to settle their

279
00:23:46,565 --> 00:23:50,565
differences, silence their complaints, and scold them into harmony and plenty.

280
00:23:50,565 --> 00:23:51,400
“he never failed to inform them”

281
00:23:51,400 --> 00:23:55,299
The entertainment of dining at Rosings was repeated about twice a week; and,

282
00:23:55,299 --> 00:23:59,099
allowing for the loss of Sir William, and there being only one card-table in the evening,

283
00:23:59,099 --> 00:24:03,900
every such entertainment was the counterpart of the first. Their other engagements were few,

284
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:08,700
as the style of living of the neighbourhood in general was beyond the Collinses’ reach. This,

285
00:24:08,700 --> 00:24:12,900
however, was no evil to Elizabeth, and upon the whole she spent her time comfortably enough:

286
00:24:12,900 --> 00:24:17,765
there were half hours of pleasant conversation with Charlotte, and the weather was so fine for

287
00:24:17,765 --> 00:24:22,065
the time of year, that she had often great enjoyment out of doors. Her favourite walk,

288
00:24:22,065 --> 00:24:26,500
and where she frequently went while the others were calling on Lady Catherine, was along the open

289
00:24:26,500 --> 00:24:31,366
grove which edged that side of the park, where there was a nice sheltered path, which no one

290
00:24:31,366 --> 00:24:36,099
seemed to value but herself, and where she felt beyond the reach of Lady Catherine’s curiosity.

291
00:24:36,099 --> 00:24:42,666
In this quiet way the first fortnight of her visit soon passed away. Easter was approaching, and the

292
00:24:42,666 --> 00:24:47,799
week preceding it was to bring an addition to the family at Rosings, which in so small a circle must

293
00:24:47,799 --> 00:24:53,099
be important. Elizabeth had heard, soon after her arrival, that Mr. Darcy was expected there in the

294
00:24:53,099 --> 00:24:57,233
course of a few weeks; and though there were not many of her acquaintance whom she did not prefer,

295
00:24:57,233 --> 00:25:01,366
his coming would furnish one comparatively new to look at in their Rosings parties,

296
00:25:01,366 --> 00:25:06,000
and she might be amused in seeing how hopeless Miss Bingley’s designs on him were, by his

297
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:10,799
behaviour to his cousin, for whom he was evidently destined by Lady Catherine, who talked of his

298
00:25:10,799 --> 00:25:15,532
coming with the greatest satisfaction, spoke of him in terms of the highest admiration, and

299
00:25:15,532 --> 00:25:19,866
seemed almost angry to find that he had already been frequently seen by Miss Lucas and herself.

300
00:25:20,633 --> 00:25:23,799
His arrival was soon known at the Parsonage; for Mr. Collins

301
00:25:23,799 --> 00:25:27,366
was walking the whole morning within view of the lodges opening into Hunsford Lane,

302
00:25:27,366 --> 00:25:28,233
in order to have

303
00:25:28,299 --> 00:25:32,265
the earliest assurance of it; and, after making his bow as the carriage turned into the park,

304
00:25:32,265 --> 00:25:36,400
hurried home with the great intelligence. On the following morning he hastened to

305
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:40,500
Rosings to pay his respects. There were two nephews of Lady Catherine to require them,

306
00:25:40,500 --> 00:25:44,933
for Mr. Darcy had brought with him a Colonel Fitzwilliam, the younger son of his uncle,

307
00:25:44,933 --> 00:25:49,565
Lord ——; and, to the great surprise of all the party, when Mr. Collins returned,

308
00:25:49,565 --> 00:25:54,466
the gentlemen accompanied him. Charlotte had seen them from her husband’s room, crossing the road,

309
00:25:54,466 --> 00:25:59,166
and immediately running into the other, told the girls what an honour they might expect, adding,—

310
00:25:59,166 --> 00:26:03,299
“I may thank you, Eliza, for this piece of civility. Mr. Darcy would

311
00:26:03,299 --> 00:26:07,599
never have come so soon to wait upon me.” Elizabeth had scarcely time to disclaim

312
00:26:07,599 --> 00:26:11,200
all right to the compliment before their approach was announced by the door-bell,

313
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:16,065
and shortly afterwards the three gentlemen entered the room. Colonel Fitzwilliam, who led the way,

314
00:26:16,065 --> 00:26:22,733
was about thirty, not handsome, but in person and address most truly the gentleman. Mr. Darcy looked

315
00:26:22,733 --> 00:26:27,000
just as he had been used to look in Hertfordshire, paid his compliments, with his usual reserve,

316
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:31,366
to Mrs. Collins; and whatever might be his feelings towards her friend, met her with every

317
00:26:31,366 --> 00:26:35,799
appearance of composure. Elizabeth merely courtesied to him, without saying a word.

318
00:26:36,765 --> 00:26:40,733
Colonel Fitzwilliam entered into conversation directly, with the readiness and ease of a

319
00:26:40,733 --> 00:26:45,532
well-bred man, and talked very pleasantly; but his cousin, after having addressed a slight

320
00:26:45,532 --> 00:26:49,900
observation on the house and garden to Mrs. Collins, sat for some time without speaking

321
00:26:49,900 --> 00:26:55,000
to anybody. At length, however, his civility was so far awakened as to inquire of Elizabeth after

322
00:26:55,000 --> 00:27:00,466
the health of her family. She answered him in the usual way; and, after a moment’s pause, added,—

323
00:27:00,466 --> 00:27:05,565
“My eldest sister has been in town these three months. Have you never happened to see her there?”

324
00:27:06,233 --> 00:27:10,366
She was perfectly sensible that he never had: but she wished to see whether he would betray

325
00:27:10,366 --> 00:27:14,265
any consciousness of what had passed between the Bingleys and Jane; and she thought he

326
00:27:14,265 --> 00:27:18,366
looked a little confused as he answered that he had never been so fortunate as to meet Miss

327
00:27:18,366 --> 00:27:23,166
Bennet. The subject was pursued no further, and the gentlemen soon afterwards went away.

328
00:27:25,599 --> 00:27:26,500
CHAPTER XXXI.
